# Quality bar (Jess)

## AST structure invariants (must never be violated)
- **No self-parenting**: `node.parent === node` is forbidden.
- **No circular parent chains**: traversing `node.parent` must never loop back to the node.
- **No node in its own children**: e.g. a Rules node cannot contain itself.
- Fix violations **at the source** (creation/manipulation), not by filtering during traversal.

## Type safety / node safety
- Do not use `as any` to work around types.
- Do not attach ad-hoc properties to nodes (e.g. `(node as any).foo = ...`).
- If data must flow between phases, use established registries/context patterns; prefer `WeakMap`s keyed by node where appropriate.

## Instrumentation safety (tests/debugging)
- Do **not** `JSON.stringify(...)` AST/parser objects (circular refs).
- Do **not** stringify nodes via `.toString()` / `.toTrimmedString()` for debugging (can affect frame state).
- Log **primitive values only** when tracing.

## Single pass over resolved shapes (no re-derivation)
A later phase consumes what an earlier phase already produced — it does **not**
re-derive it by running the earlier phase again.

- **Never re-evaluate to discover what one pass already computed.** If a feature
  needs mixins expanded, functions called, loops iterated, variables resolved, or
  selectors composed, it consumes the output of the single evaluator/render walk —
  it must not call the evaluator a second time to rebuild those facts. A "preflight"
  or "collection" phase that re-expands mixins, re-runs loops, or re-resolves
  selectors is a **defect**, not an implementation detail. (The canonical incident:
  extend's deleted `collectPlacedExtendFacts` cold twin — see `EXTEND-SEMANTICS.md`
  §1a and ledger X12.)
- **Why it is unsound, not just slow.** Evaluation has order-dependent effects
  (`@name` reassignment, `::=` optional-shadow, guard evaluation, once-only imports),
  so a second pass can silently disagree with the real output — and it multiplies
  the program's evaluation cost by the number of passes.
- **Forward references are solved by deferred rewrite, not look-ahead
  re-evaluation.** When a later result must fold into something emitted earlier,
  emit an addressable slot (a render-buffer entry, a `WeakMap`-keyed handle), record
  the fact inline during the one walk, and rewrite the slot afterward. Hold shared
  structure **by reference** (do not re-flatten or re-create nodes per use —
  especially in loop bodies, which project reused nodes); materialize a flattened
  view lazily and cached, only for the items that actually participate.
- **When you catch yourself writing a second walk that mirrors an existing
  evaluator** ("mirrors `expandCall`", "like the planner but for the main
  document"), stop — that is the brute-force anti-pattern. Design the single pass
  to record what the later phase needs, or escalate. Do not ship the mirror.
